    link: { text: 'Find lost super — ATO via myGov', url: 'https://www.ato.gov.au/individuals-and-families/super-for-individuals-and-families/super/growing-and-keeping-track-of-your-super/keep-track-of-your-super/find-your-lost-super' },
  },
  {
    id: 112, cat: 'First 3 Months', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Start building your Australian credit history',
    desc: 'NZ and Australian credit reporting systems don\'t share data. Your NZ credit history is invisible to Australian lenders — you effectively start from zero. This matters when you want a car loan, phone contract or mortgage. Build history early: get an Australian credit card (even a low-limit one), ensure all bills are paid on time, and consider a phone contract rather than prepaid. Check your Australian credit report for free once a year via Equifax (equifax.com.au), Experian (experian.com.au) or Illion. A thin credit file makes it harder to rent a property or borrow money.',
    docs: [],
    cost: 'Free to check. Credit products vary.',
    time: 'Start immediately — history takes 6–12 months to build',
    link: { text: 'Free credit report — Equifax', url: 'https://www.equifax.com.au/personal/products/credit-and-identity-products/free-credit-report' },
  },
  {
    id: 113, cat: 'First 3 Months', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Understand your tenancy rights in your Australian state',
    desc: 'Australian tenancy law has changed significantly in recent years and differs from NZ and between states. Key facts: Bond is held by a state authority (not your landlord) — typically 4 weeks. Rent increases are limited to once per year and must be by written notice. Rental bidding (paying above the listed price) is now banned in most states. Many states now restrict or ban \'no-grounds\' evictions. Pets: VIC, ACT and NT now presume pets are allowed unless the landlord has a reasonable objection. Know your rights before you sign a lease. Find your state\'s Fair Trading or Tenancy Authority for specific rules.',
    docs: [],
    cost: 'Free to research',
    time: '1 hour to read your state\'s renter guide',
    link: { text: 'Renting — Fair Work Ombudsman by state', url: 'https://www.tenantsinternational.org/australia' },
  },
  {
    id: 114, cat: 'First 3 Months', situations: ['property'], states: 'all',
    title: 'Get cross-Tasman tax advice for your NZ property situation',
    desc: 'By month 3 you\'ll have a clearer picture of your Australian income and tax position. This is the right time to get formal advice from an accountant experienced in both NZ and Australian tax. Key issues to understand: the NZ-Australia Double Taxation Agreement (DTA), NZ Bright-Line test obligations on your property, Australian foreign income disclosure requirements, NZ interest deductibility rules, and whether to file NZ and Australian returns jointly or through separate agents. The cost of good advice in year one saves far more than its fee.',
    docs: ['NZ property purchase price and date', 'NZ rental income records', 'Australian income details', 'IRD number and Australian TFN'],
    cost: 'Tax advice: $300–$600/hour for cross-Tasman specialist',
    time: 'Month 2–3 after arrival',
    link: null,
  },

  // ── FIRST YEAR IN AUSTRALIA (115–122) — NEW PHASE ────────────────────
  {
    id: 115, cat: 'First Year', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Lodge your first Australian tax return',
    desc: 'The Australian tax year runs 1 July to 30 June. If you arrived mid-year and earned income, you\'ll file a partial-year return. Due date: 31 October (or later if using a registered tax agent, typically up to 15 May of the following year). Key things to know: you declare all worldwide income as an Australian tax resident. You can claim deductions for work expenses (work from home, car use, tools and equipment, work-related education), investment expenses, and tax agent fees. Use myTax (free, via myGov) or a registered tax agent. Get a tax agent in your first year — it\'s worth it.',
    docs: ['Payment summaries / income statements from employer', 'Bank interest statements', 'Work expense receipts', 'Private health insurance statement', 'Australian and NZ income details'],
    cost: 'Free via myTax. Tax agent: $150–$350 (deductible the following year).',
    time: 'Due 31 October (or via tax agent by 15 May)',
    link: { text: 'Lodge tax return — ATO myTax', url: 'https://www.ato.gov.au/individuals-and-families/lodging-a-tax-return' },
  },
  {
    id: 116, cat: 'First Year', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Transfer your KiwiSaver to your Australian super if you haven\'t yet',
    desc: 'The trans-Tasman KiwiSaver transfer is available any time after you leave NZ, but your KiwiSaver stops accumulating contributions and government MTC after you\'ve been away 3+ months. Transferring moves the balance to your Australian super fund where it can continue to grow with Australian employer contributions. The transfer takes 6–10 weeks and requires forms from both funds. Note: once transferred, the money is subject to Australian super rules — you cannot withdraw it until Australian preservation age (60). If you\'re undecided, weigh the balance against the benefit of consolidation.',
    docs: ['KiwiSaver provider contact details and account number', 'Australian super fund USI and member number', 'Trans-Tasman transfer request forms from both funds'],
    cost: 'Free (some funds charge small admin fees)',
    time: '6–10 weeks to complete',
    link: { text: 'KiwiSaver to AU super transfer — IRD', url: 'https://www.ird.govt.nz/kiwisaver/leaving-new-zealand/kiwisaver-and-australian-super' },
  },
  {
    id: 117, cat: 'First Year', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Manage your NZ student loan repayments from Australia',
    desc: 'Your NZ student loan becomes interest-bearing the day you leave NZ permanently. For 2024–2025, the overseas interest rate is around 3–7% per year (check current rate with IRD). You must make minimum repayments based on your worldwide income or face higher penalty interest and potential legal action. IRD can pursue debts through international channels. Set up an automatic payment to IRD in your NZ internet banking — you can pay in NZD from NZ bank account or in AUD. Use the IRD repayment estimator to calculate your minimum annual obligation.',
    docs: ['Student loan account number', 'IRD number', 'Current income details'],
    cost: 'Repayments continue plus interest from departure date',
    time: 'Repayments due annually or more frequently',
    link: { text: 'Student loan — overseas obligations — IRD', url: 'https://www.ird.govt.nz/student-loans/tracking-my-student-loan/if-i-go-overseas' },
  },
  {
    id: 118, cat: 'First Year', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Update your will and superannuation beneficiary nomination',
    desc: 'Your NZ will is generally valid in Australia but it may not be optimal for an Australian resident. More critically: Australian superannuation does NOT automatically form part of your estate — it has its own separate binding death benefit nomination (BDBN) process. Without a valid BDBN, your super fund has discretion over who receives your super on death (it may not go where you want). Update your super fund\'s death benefit nomination immediately. Also review your will for Australian property, bank accounts and assets. An Australian estate planning solicitor can advise — often $300–$600 for a simple will.',
    docs: ['Existing NZ will', 'Super fund BDBN form (from your super fund)'],
    cost: 'Super BDBN: free (via your super fund online). New will: $300–$600.',
    time: 'Do this in your first year — critical for families',
    link: null,
  },
  {
    id: 119, cat: 'First Year', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Check your NZ passport validity and plan renewal timeline',
    desc: 'NZ passport renewal from overseas takes significantly longer than from NZ (8–14 weeks vs 10 working days in NZ). Adult renewal from Australia costs approximately $350–$450 NZD and must be done via the NZ Passports Office or the NZ High Commission. If your passport expires within 12 months, plan your renewal early — don\'t leave it until you need to travel. As an NZ citizen on SCV (not an Australian citizen), your NZ passport is your primary travel and identity document in Australia for visa purposes.',
    docs: ['Current NZ passport', 'Photos meeting passport standards', 'Fee payment'],
    cost: '$350–$450 NZD for adult renewal from overseas',
    time: '8–14 weeks from Australia. Plan well ahead.',
    link: { text: 'Renew NZ passport from overseas — MFAT', url: 'https://www.passports.govt.nz/getting-a-passport-overseas' },
  },
  {
    id: 120, cat: 'First Year', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Start preparing your Australian permanent residency application',
    desc: 'If you\'ve been living and working in Australia on an SCV, year one is when you should start tracking your PR eligibility timeline. Under the 2023 pathway, NZ citizens need 4 years of Australian residence and meet specific criteria. Gather your evidence now: tax returns showing Australian income, lease agreements, employment records, and any other documentation that demonstrates continued Australian residence. A registered migration agent can advise on the best pathway for your specific situation. The application fee is approximately $4,115 AUD and processing takes 12–24 months.',
    docs: ['Proof of 4 years Australian residence (tax returns, leases, employment)', 'NZ passport', 'Australian address history', 'Income evidence', 'Police clearances (both NZ and AU)'],
    cost: '~$4,115 AUD application fee (subject to change)',
    time: 'Eligible after 4 years on SCV. Start compiling evidence from day one.',
    link: { text: 'Permanent residency for NZ citizens — Home Affairs', url: 'https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/visas/getting-a-visa/visa-listing/permanent-residence-nz-citizen' },
  },
  {
    id: 121, cat: 'First Year', situations: ['property'], states: 'all',
    title: 'Review your NZ property strategy — sell, keep, or expand',
    desc: 'By your first year in Australia, you\'ll have a clearer view of whether you\'re staying long-term. This is the time to make a deliberate decision about your NZ property. Selling: NZ\'s Bright-Line Test means capital gains may be taxable depending on when you bought and sold. Keeping: ongoing compliance costs, property management fees, cross-Tasman tax complexity, and exchange rate exposure. Buying in Australia: as an NZ citizen on SCV you are considered a foreign buyer in some states for certain surcharges — check your state\'s foreign buyer rules. Get advice from a New Zealand property accountant before deciding.',
    docs: [],
    cost: 'Legal/accounting advice: $500–$1,500',
    time: 'First year — before filing NZ tax return',
    link: null,
  },
  {
    id: 122, cat: 'First Year', situations: 'all', states: 'all',
    title: 'Research the Australian citizenship pathway and timeline',
    desc: 'Australian citizenship requires: 4 years as an Australian permanent resident (including at least 12 months as a PR immediately before applying), good character (criminal check), basic English, and a genuine intention to live in or maintain a close connection with Australia. The citizenship test consists of 20 questions on Australian history, values, and government. The application fee is approximately $490 AUD. NZ citizenship can be retained — Australia allows dual citizenship with New Zealand. Planning for citizenship from early on means you\'re tracking your PR timeline correctly from the start.',
    docs: ['Australian passport (for PR evidence)', 'Police clearance certificates', 'Proof of Australian residence', 'Basic English evidence (usually implicit for NZ citizens)'],
    cost: '~$490 AUD citizenship application fee',
    time: '4 years of PR before applying. Processing 12–24 months after applying.',
    link: { text: 'Apply for Australian citizenship — Home Affairs', url: 'https://immi.homeaffairs.gov.au/citizenship/become-a-citizen' },
  },
];

const categories = [...new Set(steps.map(s => s.cat))];
const FREE_LIMIT = 122;
